; A LENGTH IS BOUNDED WITHOUT BEING KNOWN (maxlen-2026-08-28).
; inputs: 0 3 8
; expect: 0 6 36
;
; The table is a `build` sized by a PARAMETER, so nothing knows how long it is
; — `exactLen` wants a literal and there is none. What bounds the walk's
; counter is that a length cannot exceed what an `int` can count (ADR 0012),
; and on Java that a `arraylength` returns a 32-bit `int`.
;
; That is what makes `i` narrow to a host `int` on Java and stay a `long`
; everywhere else, so this case is here to check the two agree. It is also the
; first case where a buffer is FROZEN and read back by a different loop
; (frozen-2026-08-28), which is the other thing the walk depends on.
;
; Three inputs only: reduction inlines `run` at every call site, and each
; call builds a table, so more of them exhausts x86's scratch registers --
; which measures the harness rather than the compiler (see run.go).
;
; Sum of 1..n, which is n(n+1)/2.
(def run (fn (n)
  (let (build n (fn (b)
         (loop ((b b) (i 0))
           (@ge i (len b))  b
           else             (again (set b i (@add i 1)) (@add i 1)))))
    (fn (t)
      (loop ((acc 0) (i 0))
        (@ge i (len t))  acc
        else             (again (@add acc (t i)) (@add i 1)))))))
